INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(6265118, 926033, 48954, '<td valign="top">I''m glad you didn''t get any aggressive and resentful comments like i did. Congrats on the top 10.</td>', 'I''m glad you didn''t get any aggressive and resentful comments like i did. Congrats on the top 10.', '2010-12-31 06:54:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(6265315, 926043, 92994, '<td valign="top">I am still confused as to how this got disqualified.  More specifically, I''m upset with the disqualification reasons.  Nothing was added to the image or the border.  This was shot in RAW (CR2) format.  The picture is a sculpture (a very interesting one!) from Laumeier Sculpture Park, in Kirkwood, MO.<br/> <br/>1.)  Imported into Digital Photo Professional 3.8<br/>2.)  Changed picture style to landscape<br/>3.)  Brought in the left side of the histogram to the end of the shadow data<br/>4.)  Changed the levels curve from a cubic one, to a linear one (the most apparent effect)<br/>5.)  Changed the angle and cropped with the trimming and angle adjustment tool<br/>6.)  Added a marble-like border (one of the default patterns) with GIMP.<br/> <br/>I''m sorry the moderators thought this was an invalid submission, but those were, in fact, the steps taken for my final submission.  Attempt to replicate it if you wish; it is very doable in a matter of minutes.  No, this will not stop me from participating in future challenges.  I think it is a great way to exercise creativity.  I am just venting...</td>', 'I am still confused as to how this got disqualified.  More specifically, I''m upset with the disqualification reasons.  Nothing was added to the image or the border.  This was shot in RAW (CR2) format.  The picture is a sculpture (a very interesting one!) from Laumeier Sculpture Park, in Kirkwood, MO. 1.)  Imported into Digital Photo Professional 3.82.)  Changed picture style to landscape3.)  Brought in the left side of the histogram to the end of the shadow data4.)  Changed the levels curve from a cubic one, to a linear one (the most apparent effect)5.)  Changed the angle and cropped with the trimming and angle adjustment tool6.)  Added a marble-like border (one of the default patterns) with GIMP. I''m sorry the moderators thought this was an invalid submission, but those were, in fact, the steps taken for my final submission.  Attempt to replicate it if you wish; it is very doable in a matter of minutes.  No, this will not stop me from participating in future challenges.  I think it is a great way to exercise creativity.  I am just venting...', '2010-12-31 16:08:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(6265499, 926202, 98771, '<td valign="top">This is a wonderful image, congratulations on the well deserved Blue!!</td>', 'This is a wonderful image, congratulations on the well deserved Blue!!', '2010-12-31 22:32:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(6265621, 926047, 10686, '<td valign="top">JJ, you never cease to amaze me.</td>', 'JJ, you never cease to amaze me.', '2011-01-01 01:13:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(6265684, 925900, 83313, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the explanation. Quite a curious shot that only makes sense after hearing the story behind it. Afraid I can''t help with your "prime directive" question. There have been many discussions in the forums about photographing people in public places, but I don''t recall anything about if they are in distress. I was photographing a charity run a couple of years ago, and an ROTC cadet passed out on the course. I helped her walk and stay awake, but once there were other people helping I snapped a few pics. Never did anything with them however, as I felt I was there to document the FUN aspects of the event.</td>', 'Thanks for the explanation. Quite a curious shot that only makes sense after hearing the story behind it. Afraid I can''t help with your "prime directive" question. There have been many discussions in the forums about photographing people in public places, but I don''t recall anything about if they are in distress. I was photographing a charity run a couple of years ago, and an ROTC cadet passed out on the course. I helped her walk and stay awake, but once there were other people helping I snapped a few pics. Never did anything with them however, as I felt I was there to document the FUN aspects of the event.', '2011-01-01 02:03:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(6266812, 926202, 79955, '<td valign="top">Congrat Robert !!!! Just saw the win...</td>', 'Congrat Robert !!!! Just saw the win...', '2011-01-02 00:31:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(6267002, 925565, 12076, '<td valign="top">I like this!  More so since you entered it in a challenge.  This really pushes me outside of the box.  A mat and frame are in order here.</td>', 'I like this!  More so since you entered it in a challenge.  This really pushes me outside of the box.  A mat and frame are in order here.', '2011-01-02 07:54:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(6267652, 925673, 42125, '<td valign="top">Why on earth do people only vote "1" for this?<br/>This picture is surely worth more!</td>', 'Why on earth do people only vote "1" for this?This picture is surely worth more!', '2011-01-02 18:09:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(6268508, 926161, 113245, '<td valign="top">I think I gave this one of the highest scores during voting, but you didn''t do so great with votes. Go figure. My take on this is that the shot is very compelling. It is invocative, imo, of the first picture ever taken (in 1827) by Nicephore Niepce titled <i>View from the Study Window</i>. Also it is reminiscent of work by Man Ray, Lisette Model''s <i>Running Legs</i>, and Gordon Parks'' <i>The Bridge</i>. But I notice that DPC voting doesn''t always tend toward the truly aesthetic and, instead, targets the "Wow Factor". The two aren''t always in sync. <br/> <br/>Your portfolio has some great shots, too. <i>The Moon and the Mountain</i>, <i>Dunes in Black and White</i>, and <i>Summer Storm Passing Over Mt. Whitney</i>, all obviously influenced by Ansel are also quite good. Recognition from the group came with those though. Nice.<br/> <br/>I also love <i>Mud Patterns</i>. Your work is tres cool.<br/> <br/>Elsewhere, what''s up with the comments referencing "another ribbon", "a gallery and a ribbon", and "on a roll". These guys are friends, right? Being new here, I''m trying to get a handle on the scene.<br/> <br/></td>', 'I think I gave this one of the highest scores during voting, but you didn''t do so great with votes. Go figure. My take on this is that the shot is very compelling. It is invocative, imo, of the first picture ever taken (in 1827) by Nicephore Niepce titled View from the Study Window. Also it is reminiscent of work by Man Ray, Lisette Model''s Running Legs, and Gordon Parks'' The Bridge. But I notice that DPC voting doesn''t always tend toward the truly aesthetic and, instead, targets the "Wow Factor". The two aren''t always in sync.  Your portfolio has some great shots, too. The Moon and the Mountain, Dunes in Black and White, and Summer Storm Passing Over Mt. Whitney, all obviously influenced by Ansel are also quite good. Recognition from the group came with those though. Nice. I also love Mud Patterns. Your work is tres cool. Elsewhere, what''s up with the comments referencing "another ribbon", "a gallery and a ribbon", and "on a roll". These guys are friends, right? Being new here, I''m trying to get a handle on the scene. ', '2011-01-03 02:31:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(6268547, 926161, 74588, '<td valign="top">vision<br/>thats what it is</td>', 'visionthats what it is', '2011-01-03 05:08:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(6268573, 926202, 125671, '<td valign="top">The Spirit of Aggieland also refers to the "spirit can ne''er be told." Many people describe Texas A&amp;M University as having a unique school spirit that "From the outside looking in, you can''t understand it. And from the inside looking out, you can''t explain it." It has perhaps best been stated by a former Vice Student Body President of the University of Texas at Austin when discussing the loss of life in the 1999 Texas A&amp;M Bonfire collapse.<br/>=========================<br/>Earl Nunes</td>', 'The Spirit of Aggieland also refers to the "spirit can ne''er be told." Many people describe Texas A&M University as having a unique school spirit that "From the outside looking in, you can''t understand it. And from the inside looking out, you can''t explain it." It has perhaps best been stated by a former Vice Student Body President of the University of Texas at Austin when discussing the loss of life in the 1999 Texas A&M Bonfire collapse.=========================Earl Nunes', '2011-01-03 06:01:25.000000', NULL, 0),
